142:Looking at the whole sample, our findings show significant volume zscore decreases in the entire CC (and body) in MDD patients in the first episode, supporting the hypothesis that when one first becomes clinically depressed, this is accompanied by significant CC volume decreases (see Supplemental Table 1), however not as expected in the genu. This is of  interest  because  neural  activity  within  the  medioprefrontal  brain structures, structurally connected to the ventromedial parts of the brain via  the  anterior  forceps  with  the  genu  (Sarikcioglu  et  al.,  2007),  is mostly found to be increased during the depressive state (Rive et al., 2013; Wu and Baeken, 2023), reflecting its involvement in (dysfunctional) emotional regulation processes. Moreover, our mid-to-late first episode onset MDD patients (age ≥ 45y) showed similar CC volume zscore decreases (entire CC, and body). No such (sub)volumic changes were observed for early onset first episode MDD patients. Although our results do not completely support previous observations where in adult MDD (and bipolar depression) no differences in entire CC volumes were reported  (Piras  et  al.,  2021),  it  has  been  suggested  that  the  neural integrity  of  the  CC  is  compromised  only  late  life  depression  (Emsell et al., 2017), regardless of early adverse life events, such as childhood maltreatment (Ritchie et al., 2012). In line with the current observations, it has been reported that in late life depression (mean age = 72.4 y, sd = 4.8), CC (sub)volumes decreased in genu and splenium (Hahn et al., 2015), suggesting that the effects of depressive symptoms at an older age may affect these deep white matter structures more profoundly. Also in our depressed cohort the CC volume decreases were more apparent in the mid-to-late first episode MDD patients (age ≥ 45y), however, the mean age here was clearly younger (mean age = 53.15 y, sd = 6.30).
154:It has been reported that early onset MDD patients showed increases in the white matter of the corpus callosum, the corticospinal midbrain, and  the  inferior  fronto-occipital  fasciculus,  thus  distinguishing  early from late onset MDD (Cheng et al., 2014). It has been suggested that CC volume enlargements may occur in depressed patients with a family history of MDD (Lacerda et al., 2005), or greater CC fractional anisotropy  in  unaffected  first-degree  healthy  relatives  who  experienced adverse events in early life (Frodl et al., 2012). Although we had no information on potential stressful early life events, it has been documented that this could be related to depression severity and mediated by white  matter  alterations  (Wang  et  al.,  2022).  Interestingly,  stressexposed mice exhibit reduced oligodendrocyte volumes in the cortex, but  increased  oligodendrocyte  volumes  in  the  corpus  callosum  was found to be accompanied by an excess of arborisation of oligodendrocyte processes (Poletti et al., 2018). Although speculative, in assuming that severity of depression is related to higher stress levels and stress hormones at the early stages of depression may result in white matter increases, our results imply that the increased CC (sub)volumes represent a compensatory neurobiological defense mechanism due to a disturbed stress system, especially for first-episode early onset MDD patients. This also appears to be independent of whether these patients were taking antidepressants, which is in line with recent reviews that were unable to conclude  whether  antidepressant  treatment  significantly  alters  gray matter volumes (Enneking et al., 2020).
200:- Lee, S., Pyun, S.B., Choi, K.W., Tae, W.S., 2020. Shape and volumetric differences in the corpus callosum between patients with major depressive disorder and healthy controls. Psychiatry Investig. 17, 941 -950.
