When  looking  at  early  onset  (age ≤ 44  y)  and  mid-to-late  first episode onset (age ≥ 45 y) depressed patients separately, both groups did not significantly differ in depression severity scores (HAMD) ( p = 0.08). By definition the two groups significantly differed in age, however, they did not significantly differ in gender ( p = 0.24). Also, education  was  significantly  different  between  both  groups  ( p = 0.02), showing a lower level of education in the late onset first episode MDD patients. We found no significant CC (sub)volume z-scores differences between  early  and  mid-to-late  onset  first  episode  depression  (see Table 2).

On the other hand, relative to the norms computed by the healthy controls,  we  found  that  mid-to-late  onset  first  episode  MDD  showed significant CC volume z-scores decreases for the entire CC ( p = 0.02) as well as for the body of the CC ( p = 0.01, FDR corrected). Furthermore, although we did not find significant CC (sub)volume differences for the early onset first episode MDD patients, depression severity scores were significantly associated with increased entire CC volume z-scores ( r = 0.15, p = 0.01), including body ( r = 0.15, p = 0.03, FDR corrected) and splenium ( r = 0.14, p = 0.03, FDR corrected). For an overview, see Table 2 and Fig. 3. For additional information see also Supplemental Fig. 3.

